Comment:
 These binaries are statically linked and compiled from Bazel-fetched
 sources, so the licenses above are the union of what is built into them;
 not every license applies to every binary.
 .
 Conversion data (mozc_server only): the open-source Mozc dictionary
 (IPAdic-derived vocabulary under NAIST terms, ICOT Free Software
 conditions, Okinawa Dictionary public-domain named-entity enrichments,
 and Google extras). Provenance matches src/data/dictionary_oss/README.txt
 and the top-level LICENSE.
 .
 Third-party code, with the upstream version pinned in src/MODULE.bazel:
  - Protocol Buffers, BSD-3-Clause (Google).
  - Abseil, Apache-2.0 (Google).
  - darts-clone 0.32h, BSD-2-Clause (Susumu Yata).
  - marisa-trie 0.2.6, offered by its author under BSD-2-Clause or
    LGPL-2.1+; marinaMoji elects BSD-2-Clause.
  - Japanese usage dictionary (Komatsu et al.), BSD-2-Clause.
  - RapidJSON 1.1.0, MIT (Tencent, Milo Yip). Only the library headers are
    used; bin/jsonchecker (the sole JSON-license part) and the msinttypes
    BSD files (Windows-only) are not built into this package.
  - libsodium 1.0.19, ISC (mozc_sync only).
  - zlib, Zlib license (Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler).
  - OpenCC 1.2.0, Apache-2.0.
 .
 Data compiled into mozc_server, besides the dictionary above:
  - Postal codes (KEN_ALL and JIGYOSYO CSV snapshots, fetched by Bazel from
    github.com/hiroyuki-komatsu/japanpost_zipcode, which mirrors Japan
    Post's downloads): Japan Post Co., Ltd. states that it does not claim
    copyright in this data and that it may be distributed freely (see the
    Japan-Post-Postal-Code-Data license). Debian's mozc package ships the
    same data and records it as CC0-1.0.
  - Emoji (src/data/emoji/emoji_data.tsv): generated by Mozc's own tool
    (tools/emoji/generate_emoji_data.py upstream) and updated from the
    Unicode CLDR (last: CLDR 48, Emoji 17.0). The Unicode names and CLDR
    annotations are (c) Unicode, Inc. under the Unicode License v3
    (Unicode-3.0); the readings are Mozc's own (BSD-3-Clause).
  - Emoticons (src/data/emoticon): Google's original data, imported into
    the open-source Mozc repository in 2010 (BSD-3-Clause, as for the
    rest of the Google sources). Debian's mozc package covers it the same
    way.
 The Qt, GTK and IBus libraries are linked dynamically and are not
 redistributed in this package.


